# Suppression entries for scripts/check-public-repo-secrets.py.
#
# Three forms, narrowest first — prefer the narrowest that works:
#   path/to/file.ext:128       ONE line of one file
#   path/to/file.ext::PII      ONE category, whole file
#   path/to/file.ext           whole file, EVERY category  (avoid)
#
# The bare form was the only one available until hoiboy-uk#56, and every entry
# below used it. Each had been added to silence one specific self-reference, and
# each silently disabled all six categories for that whole file as a side
# effect — a live platform token inside any of them was reported clean. They are
# category-scoped now. Path matching is on whole segments and anchored at the
# end, so an entry no longer travels to a different file that merely contains it
# as a substring.

# Self-reference: the secret detection script carries its own patterns as string
# literals, plus example addresses and phone numbers in the PII docstrings.
scripts/check-public-repo-secrets.py::PRIVATE_KEY
scripts/check-public-repo-secrets.py::GENERIC_SECRET
scripts/check-public-repo-secrets.py::PII

# Import script contains source paths for legacy blog migration.
scripts/import_aam_sql.py::PRIVATE_PATH
scripts/import_aam_sql.py::GENERIC_SECRET

# Research docs reference deployment tokens as examples (not real values) and
# name the operator-facing alert address in the Pages bindings table.
docs/research/09_DEPLOYMENT.md::GENERIC_SECRET
docs/research/09_DEPLOYMENT.md::PII

# `scripts/sst3_mirror_utils.py` was listed here for the same self-reference
# reason. Measured at hoiboy-uk#56: it produces ZERO findings in any category,
# so the entry suppressed nothing and has been removed rather than carried as
# dead config that would silently cover a future real finding.

# Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon token — PUBLIC by design (ships in every
# page's rendered HTML; it is a site identifier, not a credential). Precise
# file:line so the rest of head.html is still secret-scanned.
# This entry is LINE-ANCHORED on purpose, so the rest of head.html keeps being
# scanned. The cost is that any insertion above the beacon moves it and the
# entry goes stale, which has now happened three times: line 101 after the
# branded default-card comment (#52), line 110 after the noindex meta block
# (blog-priv#55 Ralph round 15), line 124 after the 404 noindex-by-Kind guard
# (blog-priv#64 Stage 5), line 128 after the font ital-axis comment (agit-ops#3).
# If you edit head.html above this tag, re-run
# `python3 scripts/check-public-repo-secrets.py layouts/_partials/head.html`
# and update the number here. It fails loud, so it cannot drift silently.
layouts/_partials/head.html:128

# --- Published content carrying third-party contact details -----------------
# These WERE whole-file `::PII` suppressions covering blog posts and pages whose
# published text legitimately includes a venue's address or phone number.
#
# They are replaced by exact VALUE entries in .secret-pii-allowlist, for two
# reasons. A whole-file entry switched PII detection off for that file entirely
# and permanently, so a genuinely NEW address appearing in one of these
# voice-sacred posts would have been reported clean. And the built-output scan
# sees these details in `public/blogs/<slug>/index.html` rather than in the
# content source, so a path-scoped entry cannot cover both surfaces without
# being duplicated per surface and re-verified on every rebuild.
#
# `5-common-mistakes-dance-teachers` produced no PII finding in either the source
# or the rendered tree when measured, so it needed no replacement entry and is
# simply gone rather than carried as dead config.

# Upstream SIL Open Font License text for VT323, carrying the type designer's
# contact address. Vendored verbatim; modifying it would break the licence.
scripts/social-cards/fonts/VT323-OFL.txt::PII
